    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain why some earthquakes cause more damage than others.', marks: 5, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Magnitude determines the energy released; higher magnitude earthquakes cause more ground shaking (1). Shallow focus earthquakes cause more surface damage than deep focus ones (1). Population density affects how many people and structures are at risk (1). Building quality and earthquake-resistant design determine structural damage (1). Preparedness, warning systems and emergency response capacity influence the scale of casualties (1).', marks_scheme: '1 mark: Higher magnitude earthquakes release more energy and cause more ground shaking. 1 mark: Shallow focus earthquakes cause more surface damage than deep focus ones. 1 mark: Higher population density increases the number of people and structures at risk. 1 mark: Poor building quality increases structural damage. 1 mark: Preparedness, warning systems and emergency response influence the scale of casualties.' },
      { q: 'Describe how a tsunami is generated and propagated.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'An underwater earthquake causes vertical displacement of the seabed (1). This displaces a large volume of water creating waves that radiate outward (1). In deep ocean, waves travel at high speed but have low height; as they approach shallow coastal waters they slow down, compress and increase in height (1). The wave can reach heights of tens of metres when it breaks onshore (1).', diagram: true, marks_scheme: '1 mark: An underwater earthquake causes vertical displacement of the seabed. 1 mark: Seabed displacement creates waves that radiate outward. 1 mark: In deep ocean waves travel fast but low; in shallow water they slow, compress and increase in height. 1 mark: The wave can reach heights of tens of metres when it breaks onshore.' },
      { q: 'Explain why people continue to live in areas at risk from tectonic hazards.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Volcanic soils are extremely fertile for agriculture, providing livelihoods (1). Economic opportunities from tourism, geothermal energy and mining attract settlement (1). Cultural and family ties bind communities to their ancestral lands (1). Perception of risk may be low if events are infrequent, or people may feel prepared and protected (1).', marks_scheme: '1 mark: Volcanic soils are extremely fertile, providing agricultural livelihoods. 1 mark: Economic opportunities from tourism, geothermal energy and mining attract settlement. 1 mark: Cultural and family ties bind communities to their ancestral lands. 1 mark: Perception of risk may be low if events are infrequent or people feel prepared.' },
      { q: 'Explain how governance affects the impact of natural hazards.', marks: 5, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Effective governance enables investment in early warning systems that save lives through timely evacuation (1). Enforced building codes ensure structures can withstand hazard events (1). Land use planning prevents development in high-risk zones (1). Coordination of emergency services provides rapid response (1). Corruption or weak governance can undermine all of these, increasing vulnerability (1).', marks_scheme: '1 mark: Effective governance enables investment in early warning systems. 1 mark: Enforced building codes ensure structures can withstand hazard events. 1 mark: Land use planning prevents development in high-risk zones. 1 mark: Coordination of emergency services provides rapid response. 1 mark: Corruption or weak governance can undermine all measures, increasing vulnerability.' },
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that vulnerability rather than magnitude determines the impact of natural hazards.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Evidence supporting: The 2010 Haiti earthquake (M7.0) killed over 200,000 due to poverty, poor construction and weak governance, while the 2011 Japan earthquake (M9.0) caused fewer direct deaths due to preparedness and infrastructure (2). Poverty concentrates populations in hazardous locations with inadequate shelter and limited capacity to prepare or recover (2). However, extremely high magnitude events can overwhelm even well-prepared societies, as shown by the 2011 Japanese tsunami which breached defences despite advanced warning systems (2). In conclusion, vulnerability amplifies impact at any magnitude, but both factors interact; the most devastating disasters occur when high magnitude events strike highly vulnerable populations (2).', marks_scheme: 'Level 3 (6-8 marks): balanced evaluation with named examples and conclusion. Level 2 (3-5 marks): some developed points. Level 1 (1-2 marks): basic points. Credit: Haiti vs Japan earthquake comparison; role of poverty and governance in vulnerability; high magnitude events overwhelming prepared societies; conclusion on interaction of both factors.' },
      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of different strategies for managing tectonic hazards.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Prediction and monitoring: Volcanic eruptions can be forecasted through seismic activity and gas emissions, but earthquake prediction remains unreliable, limiting this approach (2). Engineering solutions: Earthquake-resistant buildings and sea walls reduce casualties but are expensive and not available in all contexts; they can also create false security (2). Planning and preparedness: Education, evacuation plans and building codes are cost-effective but require sustained political commitment and community engagement; international frameworks like the Sendai Framework provide guidance but implementation varies (2).', marks_scheme: 'Level 3 (6-8 marks): balanced evaluation with named examples and conclusion. Level 2 (3-5 marks): some developed points. Level 1 (1-2 marks): basic points. Credit: prediction/monitoring strengths and limitations; engineering solutions cost and effectiveness; planning and preparedness including Sendai Framework; overall assessment of which strategies are most effective.' },
    ],
